A||||9||Getaway2k||Gas guage problem||||5||
Z||000000||Getaway2k||08-03-2000||08:19 PM||||My gas guage is reading empty at 6 o'clock and full at 12 o'clock. It seems to have also become sensative to the engine idle (almost like a tach!). While the car is idling the guage will rest at the half way mark (aka 6 o'clock). If I rev the engine the needle will rise 180 degrees (aka 12 o'clock). The guage will not drop below the half mark at any time. I swear this happens while I'm sober (however, the pink elephant and the green gremlin say they saw it too). Any idea's?||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-03-2000||09:05 PM||||does your gage go down as you use up the gas. if it does, you can remove the clear faceplate by taking out the four star screws and i think there either 2 or 4 small 7mm screws under the edge. then you can just pull off the needle and set it where you need it. Sounds like you could have a bad connector on the gage too, with face out you can pull out gage and clean the contact where it pushes in.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Getaway2k||08-03-2000||09:38 PM||||The empty mark is now at the half tank mark and the full mark is at high noon! How do I remove the guage to clean/inspect the connector?||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||08-04-2000||08:47 AM||||I had a problem similar to this and it turned out that the fuel pump wiring harness connector (which also has the wire for the gas gauge) had melted.  This 3 pin connector is on the forward engine compartment wall near the center.  I would check that too. <P>------------------<BR>Dillon - Titusville, FL<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"Matched Perfect and Staggered Special"||||reg||1
Z||000004||squid||08-04-2000||11:46 AM||||Most GM cars are 0 ohms full and 90 ohms empty. Check at the yellow 3 pin connector. An intermittent short in the wiring like DJRice described would definately cause this.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||08-04-2000||11:56 AM||||I had a similar problem.  It would show full when it was full, but then about 3/4 of a tank it would drop to empty.  Then at a 1/4 tank it would go back to being accurate.  It turned out to be the fuel pump.  Is your pump pretty noisey or run longer than it used too? I can't hardly hear my new one and it's not on long at all.  It cost me  about 300.00 bucks at a dealership to get it changed.  In Sacramento.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Getaway2k||08-04-2000||08:01 PM||||Can you give me a little more detail on where/how to check the connector that you guys are referring to (sorry, I'm mechanically challenged!)? I can hear the fuel pump quite well when I turn the key to the "on" position, after that I cannot hear it at all. To change the fuel pump I have to drop the tank, right? Also, prior to this the gas guage did the exact same thing that you mentioned. After filling the tank the needle would drop to 1/2 tank then when I caught up to the guage, and really had a 1/2 tank, the guage would work fine.<P>Thanks for the replies everyone!<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://pages.prodigy.net/nuppy/getaway.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Getaway2k||08-04-2000||08:10 PM||||One more thing, assuming I have to change the fuel pump, will placing the car on standard height jackstands give me enough room to drop the tank and remove it from under the car? This sounds like it's going to be as bad as changing the alternator!<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://pages.prodigy.net/nuppy/getaway.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Getaway2k||08-06-2000||06:35 PM||||HELP!!!<P>I have the guage cluster out and am in the process of cleaning the gas guage contacts. In the event I need a new gas guage, does anyone know where I can buy a new one? Is there any way to check the guage for damage while it's out of the car? A new A/C Delco pump will cost me $60.00 at Autozone, however, they do not carry the sending unit (I'd like to replace this too if I'm going to take the tank out). Anyone know where I can get a new sending unit? Also, I figured I'd replace the feed and return fuel lines while I'm under there. <P>Thanks everybody!<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://pages.prodigy.net/nuppy/getaway.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Getaway2k (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000009||Oreif||08-06-2000||09:54 PM||||You can get Fuel sending units from a dealer or I think wrenchhead.com has them. Caution these are VERY expensive. Dealer wants $325.00 and I think wrenchhead wanted $285.00<BR>||||reg||1
